WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.391
Reading Cisco's advisories together
gives a floor: a minimum release per

2
00:00:04.391 --> 00:00:08.950
software train, one for the Manager and
one for the Controller and Validator.

3
00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:13.788
That's the program's inference from Cisco's
tables, so re-read them at build time.

4
00:00:13.788 --> 00:00:15.150
A few things stand out.

5
00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:20.275
Several older trains have no fix of their
own, and Cisco's answer is to migrate.

6
00:00:20.275 --> 00:00:23.326
The newest train isn't
listed for the Controller

7
00:00:23.326 --> 00:00:26.300
and Validator in three
advisories, so ask Cisco.

8
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:32.173
And the edges have their own catalog
entries, for the web interface and SNMP,

9
00:00:32.173 --> 00:00:37.288
so the HTTP server stays off and
SNMPv3 sits behind an access list.

10
00:00:37.288 --> 00:00:39.988
Cisco's Live Protect
shields can bridge a gap,

11
00:00:39.988 --> 00:00:43.126
but Cisco calls them
temporary partial protection.

12
00:00:43.126 --> 00:00:44.500
Next, the hunt.
